    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Denza Launching \"God's Eye\" Driving Assistance in Europe, the Z9GT Will Get It First\nThe Only 2004 Mercedes-Benz CLK DTM AMG Owned by F1 Champion Jenson Button Could Sell for $660K\n12 photos\nPhoto: Iconic Auctioneers\nGerman carmaker Mercedes-Benz is not one to release special, limited-edition versions of its cars all that often. This reality makes such vehicles released in the past worth a lot of money. Especially when one of them has a long connection with a former Formula 1 champion.\nThe car I’m talking about is something called the Mercedes-Benz CLK DTM AMG , a special vehicle that was in production in the mid-2000s as a means for the German carmaker to celebrate its undisputed dominance in the Deutsche Tourenwagen Masters (DTM) at the time.\nThe model, inspired by the championship-winning competition car driven in DTM, was put together with help from the same guys who ran the works cars in the racing series, HWA AG. Only 100 of them were ever made, each powered by a supercharged 5.4-litre V8 engine that cranked out 574 horsepower and could hit 62 mph from a standstill in 3.9 seconds, on its way to a top speed of 199 mph (320 kph).\nAs soon as it was made, one of the CLK DTM AMG cars, a 2004 example, was delivered to F1 driver Jenson Button. The man seems to have really liked the car, as he kept on to it for 15 years. Granted, he didn’t drive it all that much during this time, as the vehicle shows only a little over 18,100 miles (11,300 km) of use.\nButton let go of the car in 2019, when it was sold to a Japanese collector. It is that buyer who will now be letting go of the car as well, during the Iconic Auctioneers sale at the NEC Classic Motor Show in the UK in mid-November.\nPresently in stock form, with its carbon-fiber bodywork, distinct flared arches, large wing at the rear, and the two-seat interior with motorsport influences, the 2004 Mercedes-Benz CLK DTM AMG is selling with a lot of accompanying paperwork, including servicing papers from before being sold to the Japanese, with the Guernsey Registration Certificate in Jenson Button's name, and documentation confirming its technical specification.\nAlso thrown in the package are the original CLK DTM owner's wallet, two keys, complete tool kit, tire compressor, oil filter, and first aid kit.\nThat’s the full package, if you will, but probably not what makes this car worth an estimated £500,000 (about $663,000 at today’s exchange rates). What makes it so is that it was owned by Button for most of his F1 career, from his early days at BAR-Honda to his move to McLaren late in his career.\nIn between, the car was in the driver’s possession when he won the World Championship in the colors of Brawn GP.\nI will keep an eye on the Jenson Button-owned 2004 Mercedes-Benz CLK DTM AMG and report back once I learn how much it went for.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMercedes-Benz mercedes-benz clk dtm amg Jenson Button\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2027 Chevrolet Colorado Order Guide Confirms ZR2 Desert Boss Edition's Return",
